SWEET dev overlay zlib entries (patch 0163, on top of 0117): archive version 2 lets an entry carry a zlib stream.
Version 1 archives behave exactly as before. A version 2 entry header adds u64 usize | u32 flags (0 stored, 1 zlib) | u32 0
and its crc covers the uncompressed bytes; usize <= 40 MiB per entry and in total. sd_stream (host-tested,
tools/test_devoverlay_kernel.py with host zlib) inflates through the kernel zlib (libkern/zlib.h) in 1 MiB pieces into
<path>.sweettmp, cuts off at usize (bomb), requires the stream to end exactly at the stored length, then checks length
and crc; only then sd_one renames. On a mismatch the tmp file is removed, the destination is untouched, "refused".
Identical-skip compares the inflated bytes (sd_same_z). Why: the bootloader refuses a payload of >= 32.7 MB.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/sweet_devoverlay.c b/bsd/dev/sweet_devoverlay.c
--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_devoverlay.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_devoverlay.c
@@ -9,6 +9,13 @@
  *   per entry, starting 16-byte aligned:
  *     u16 pathlen (1..1000) | u16 mode (<= 0777) | u32 uid | u32 gid | u32 crc32(data) | u64 size
  *     path (pathlen bytes, no NUL), zero pad to 16, data (size bytes), zero pad to 16
+ * Version 2 (patch 0163, written by mkdevoverlay.py only when some entry is compressed; version 1 is unchanged):
+ *   per entry 40 bytes: the 24 above, crc32 and size meaning crc32 of the UNCOMPRESSED bytes and the STORED length,
+ *     then u64 usize (uncompressed length) | u32 flags (0 stored: usize == size, 1 zlib stream) | u32 0
+ *   usize <= 40 MiB per entry and in total. A zlib entry is inflated in 1 MiB pieces straight into <path>.sweettmp;
+ *   it is renamed over <path> only if the stream ends exactly at the end of the stored bytes, produced exactly usize
+ *   bytes (never more reach the file: a "bomb" is cut off at usize) and the crc matches; otherwise the tmp file is
+ *   removed, the destination is untouched and the entry counts as refused. Identical-skip compares inflated bytes.
  * The kernel never trusts the archive: the whole structure is walked first (magic, version, count,
  * every length against the remaining bytes, no overflow) and nothing is written if that fails.
  * A bad crc or path only skips that entry.
